// NEGATIVE TEST -- `char` casts only to `i64`, and only through `as` (N4-04).
//
// The cast set was widened to admit `char` when the type landed, and widened
// too far: MEASURED, `'a' as bool` compiled and produced true, `3.7 as char`
// produced 3, and `true as char` produced 1. All four pairings type-checked
// because the check was a PREDICATE over both sides ("is each of these
// castable?") rather than a relation over the pair.
//
// The rule the surrounding code already states is that `as` covers the
// conversions with an unambiguous meaning in the target language. These have
// none. Is a letter truthy? Which character is three-tenths of the way past
// `\u{3}`? The specification answers neither, so they are refused by name
// rather than guessed at -- the same reason `String as i64` is refused.
//
// What remains legal is `char` <-> `i64`, because the code point IS the
// correspondence a character is defined by. tests/02_types_chars.pd round-trips
// it in both directions.
//
// The four forbidden cells have a fixture each, one per direction: this file
// (char->bool), bool_does_not_cast_to_char.pd, float_does_not_cast_to_char.pd
// and char_does_not_cast_to_float.pd.
fn main() {
    let c: char = 'a';
    let b: bool = c as bool;
    if b {
        print("unreachable");
    }
}
